{
  "requirement": "A provider failure that ends the model conversation returns\n`ValidationError::Provider` with the underlying I/O error to the awaiting ask builder.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The requirement has one obligation. When a provider failure ends the model conversation, the awaiting ask builder gets `ValidationError::Provider` carrying the underlying I/O error.\n\nTwo marked tests cover it:\n\n- `crates/trust-but-verify/src/agent/tests.rs#fn[assistant_text_and_provider_failure_cannot_become_typed_results]` builds `ToolAgent::new(Script::new(vec![None]))`. The `Script` provider's `complete` returns `io::Error::other(\"permanent provider failure\")` for a `None` turn. The test awaits `ctx.ask::<bool>()` and asserts the result is `Err(ValidationError::Provider(error))` with `error.to_string()` containing \"permanent provider failure\". That checks both the variant and that the underlying I/O error is carried through. It also asserts `ctx.dependencies()` is empty, so the failed ask records nothing.\n- `crates/trust-but-verify/src/agent/configured.rs#mod[tests]/fn[configured_agents_initialize_only_when_asked_and_report_setup_errors]` awaits `validator.verify` with a missing credential environment variable. It asserts `ValidationError::Provider(_)` and that the message names the credential variable. This is a second failure origin, a setup error, reaching the awaiting ask.\n\nI saw no distinct input class that the text separates. The text names no mid-conversation versus first-request distinction, so I did not require one. The retry path for transient failures is a different obligation that this requirement does not cover. Both tests carry `{test}` references to the subject, so there is no marking gap.",
  "references": "0fbc2bd5fac34b56faf1a32f5f18e07e5f7165136fc32734f4466834b4898eda",
